{"page_content": "Sediment and soil adsorption/desorption isotherm (OPPTS 835.1220) Screening method according to US-EPA guideline (OPPTS, 1996) using three soil types. Batch equilibrium method (OECD TG 106; EU C.18: Absorption \u2014 Desorption Using a Batch Equilibrium Method (Updated Guideline, adopted 21 January 2000) Test uses a range of actual soils and so represents a more realistic scenario than the HPLC (OECD 121) method. OECD TG 312: Leaching in Soil Columns (Original Guideline, adopted 13 April 2004) Ka values can be derived from column leaching studies. Used for substances with Koc values that cannot be reliably determined using other techniques (e.g. surfactants). Requires a quantitative analytical method for the substance, reliable over the range of test concentrations. For ionisable substances, soil types should cover a wide range of pH. Adjustments for poorly soluble substances given in the test guideline. Appropriate study design to estimate Ka values particularly for unstable test substances that degrade significantly during the equilibrium time of \u2018shake flask\u2019 sorption studies", "metadata": {"doc_id": "4ae74147-1754-48ad-a74f-6d0487de7fcf", "content_type": "Table", "filename": "information_requirements_r7a_en_part_1.pdf"}}